\n",")\n","\n","# Get positional embeddings\n","final_embeddings = pos_embedding(\n","    embeddings\n",")"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":52,"id":"e84222ca","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.670399Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.67004Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.680927Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.680157Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.022659,"end_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.682693+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.660034+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":[" Token ID  Position   Token  Dim 1  Dim 2  Dim 3  Dim 4  Dim 5\n","       22         0     The  -0.00   0.70  -1.46   0.90  -0.60\n","       24         1     cat   0.83   0.78   0.16   1.76   1.10\n","       32         2     sat  -0.99  -0.19   0.08   0.65   0.29\n","       31         3      on   1.75  -3.47  -0.34  -0.20   0.81\n","       28         4     the  -1.13   0.38  -0.59   1.63  -0.97\n","       25         5     mat  -0.62   1.00   0.54   2.92   1.02\n","       50         6 because   0.44   1.00   1.46   1.24  -1.97\n","       30         7      it  -0.08   0.86   0.52   1.95  -0.46\n","       29         8     was   1.95   1.47   1.65   1.25  -0.21\n","       34         9   tired   1.70  -0.86  -1.32   1.73   0.78\n","        1        10       .  -1.78  -0.88  -1.36   0.22   1.66\n"]}],"source":["# Create embedding data\n","data = final_embeddings[0].detach().numpy()\n","\n","df = pd.DataFrame(\n","    data,\n","    columns=[f\"Dim {i+1}\" for i in range(data.shape[1])]\n",")\n","\n","# Add Token ID and Word columns and Word position\n","df.insert(0, \"Token ID\", output.ids)\n","df.insert(1, \"Position\", range(max_len))\n","df.insert(2, \"Token\", tokens)\n","\n","# Display table\n","print(df.round(2).to_string(index=False))"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"da67d6d8","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.009102,"end_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.700935+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.691833+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["This matrix is what gets passed to the next Transformer layer. The model now receives vectors containing both token information and positional information.\n","\n","### Learned positional encoding\n","\n","Position vectors are stored in a trainable embedding table.\n","\n","- Position 0 has a trainable vector.\n","- Position 1 has another trainable vector.\n","- The model learns these vectors during training.\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":53,"id":"5b91aac5","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.720624Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.720295Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.725965Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.724981Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.017415,"end_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.727425+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.71001+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["import torch\n","import torch.nn as nn\n","\n","class LearnablePositionalEmbedding(nn.Module):\n","    def __init__(self, max_seq_len, d_model):\n","        super().__init__()\n","\n","        self.position_embedding = nn.Embedding(\n","            max_seq_len, d_model\n","        )\n","\n","    def forward(self, x):\n","        # x shape: (batch_size, seq_len, d_model)\n","\n","        seq_len = x.size(1)\n","\n","        positions = torch.arange(\n","            seq_len, device=x.device\n","        )\n","\n","        pos_emb = self.position_embedding(positions)\n","\n","        return x + pos_emb"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"13ff91d4","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.009143,"end_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.745926+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.736783+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["### Relative positional encoding\n","\n","Represents the relative distance between tokens rather than relying only on their absolute positions.\n","\n","Example: Is one token 2 positions before another?\n","\n","\n","### Rotary Positional Embedding (RoPE)\n","\n","Encodes positional information by rotating components of Query and Key vectors.\n","\n","- Widely used in modern decoder-only LLMs.\n","- Incorporates relative positional information into attention.\n","\n","### ALiBi (Attention with Linear Biases)\n","\n","Adds distance-dependent biases to attention scores.\n","\n","- Does not add positional vectors to token embeddings.\n","- Encourages attention to account for token distance.\n","\n","## Sinusoidal vs Learned Positional Encoding\n","\n","Let's compare the two most fundamental approaches.\n","\n","| Feature                            | Sinusoidal               | Learned                   |\n","| ---------------------------------- | ------------------------ | ------------------------- |\n","| Position vectors                   | Mathematically generated | Learned during training   |\n","| Trainable parameters               | No                       | Yes                       |\n","| Generalization to unseen positions | Can be calculated        | Depends on implementation |\n","| Computational cost                 | Low                      | Low                       |\n","| Original Transformer               | Yes                      | No                        |\n","\n","## What happens after positional encoding?\n","\n","Now let's connect positional encoding to self-attention.\n","\n","Suppose our sentence is:\n","\n","`The cat sat on the mat.`\n","\n","After tokenization, embedding and positional encoding, we have a sequence of vectors:\n","\n","$$ X=E+PE $$\n","\n","These vectors enter the self-attention mechanism.\n","\n","Self-attention calculates three matrices:\n","\n","$$ Q=XW_Q $$\n","\n","$$ K=XW_K $$\n","\n","$$ V=XW_V $$\n","\n","Where:\n","\n","- $Q$ = Query matrix.\n","- $K$ = Key matrix.\n","- $V$ = Value matrix.\n","- $W_Q,W_K,W_V$ = Trainable weight matrices.\n","\n","Then attention is calculated using:\n","\n","$$ Attention(Q,K,V)= softmax\\left(\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right)V $$\n","\n","Because positional information was already added to $X$, the Query and Key representations can incorporate information about token positions.\n","\n","For example, the model can learn that:\n","\n","- `cat` is the subject of `sat`.\n","- `sat` is related to `mat`.\n","- The word `on` connects `sat` and `the mat`.\n","\n","Positional information helps the attention mechanism distinguish relationships that depend on word order.\n","\n","**Important:** Positional encoding does not itself determine grammar or sentence meaning. The Transformer learns those relationships during training.\n","\n","## Interview Questions\n","\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>What is positional encoding?</summary>\n","\n","Positional encoding is a technique used in Transformers to provide information about the order and position of tokens in a sequence. It is necessary because self-attention alone does not inherently represent token order.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Why does a Transformer need positional encoding?</summary>\n","\n","A Transformer needs positional encoding because self-attention does not inherently understand the order of tokens in a sequence. Positional encoding provides information about token positions, helping the model understand the sequence order.\n","\n","</details>\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Why does the original Transformer use sine and cosine?</summary>\n","\n","The original Transformer uses sine and cosine because they generate position-dependent patterns at different frequencies. These patterns help the model distinguish token positions and understand relative positional offsets.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Is positional encoding trainable?</summary>\n","\n","It depends on the approach:\n","\n","- Sinusoidal positional encoding: Not trainable.\n","- Learned positional encoding: Trainable.\n","- RoPE: Uses predefined position-dependent rotations, although some variants introduce additional learned components.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>What is the difference between positional encoding and token embedding?</summary>\n","\n","Token embedding represents the semantic information (meaning) of a token, whereas positional encoding represents the position of a token in a sequence.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>What is the difference between absolute and relative positional encoding?</summary>\n","\n","Absolute positional encoding represents the position of a token in a sequence, whereas relative positional encoding represents the distance or positional relationship between tokens.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Does positional encoding happen before or after self-attention?</summary>\n","\n","In the original Transformer, positional encoding is added to token embeddings before they enter the self-attention layers.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","# Self Attention\n","\n","Encoder Self-Attention is one of the most important mechanisms in the Transformer architecture. It allows the model to understand how each word in a sentence relates to every other word in that same sentence.\n","\n","Imagine the following sentence:\n","\n","> The cat sat on the mat because it was tired.\n","\n","When we read this sentence, we understand that the word `it` refers to the cat.\n","\n","How?\n","\n","> Our brain considers the relationship between different words in the sentence.\n","\n","A Transformer needs a mechanism to understand these relationships. That's where Self-Attention comes in.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","- `it` needs to pay attention to `cat`.\n","- `tired` needs to understand the context of `it`.\n","- `sat` may need to pay attention to `cat`.\n","- `mat` may relate to `sat`.\n","\n","Self-Attention allows every token to examine every other token and determine which tokens are important for understanding its meaning.\n","\n","![Attention Mechanism](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/attention-mechanism.png)\n","\n","The key idea:\n","\n","> Every token looks at all the tokens in the input sequence, calculates their relevance, and creates a new contextual representation of itself.\n","\n","For example, the representation of `it` will contain information about `cat`, because `cat` is relevant to understanding `it`.\n","\n","## Self Attention in Encoder\n","\n","The encoder generally contains these components:\n","\n","1. Input Embedding\n","2. Positional Encoding\n","3. Multi-Head **Self-Attention**\n","4. Add & Normalize\n","5. Feed-Forward Neural Network\n","6. Add & Normalize\n","\n","Our focus is step 3: Encoder Self-Attention.\n","\n","Before self-attention begins, each token already has:\n","\n","- An embedding vector representing its learned meaning.\n","- Positional information representing its position in the sentence.\n","\n","These vectors are passed into the self-attention mechanism.\n","\n","## Components\n","\n","This is the most important concept to understand.\n","\n","Self-attention uses three vectors:\n","\n","1. Query (Q)\n","2. Key (K)\n","3. Value (V)\n","\n","Imagine you enter a library and want to find a book about Machine Learning.\n","\n","Query (Q): What am I looking for?\n","\n","> I want to learn Machine Learning.\n","\n","Key (K): What information does each book advertise?\n","\n","> Each book has a title, keywords, or description.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","- Book A: Machine Learning Fundamentals\n","- Book B: Python Programming\n","- Book C: Database Management\n","\n","You compare your query with the information associated with each book.\n","\n","Value (V): What information does the book actually contain?\n","\n","> After determining which books are relevant, you retrieve their actual content.\n","\n","Now map this to Transformer:\n","\n","| Component | Meaning                                              |\n","| --------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n","| Query     | What information is this token looking for?          |\n","| Key       | What information does this token offer for matching? |\n","| Value     | What information should this token contribute?       |\n","\n","For example, consider the token `it`.\n","\n","- Its Query asks: Which other tokens provide useful contextual information?\n","- Every token has a Key that can be compared with this Query.\n","- Every token has a Value containing information that can be combined into the final representation.\n","\n","Important: Query, Key, and Value are not manually written by developers. The Transformer `learns them through trainable weight matrices`.\n","\n","## Calculating Query, Key, and Value\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":54,"id":"a403439c","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.766906Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.766628Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.771055Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.770094Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.017856,"end_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.77272+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.754864+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["\n","Original text: The cat sat on the mat because it was tired.\n","Tokens: ['The', 'cat', 'sat', 'on', 'the', 'mat', 'because', 'it', 'was', 'tired', '.']\n"]}],"source":["print(\"\\nOriginal text:\", text)\n","print(\"Tokens:\", tokens)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"009a8c06","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.009144,"end_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.790792+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:46.781648+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["After token embedding and positional encoding, each token has a 5-dimensional vector.\n"]},{"cell_type":"c