  The         0   0.49  -0.10  -0.81   0.56  -0.57\n","       24     cat         1   0.60  -0.11  -0.61   0.71  -0.71\n","       32     sat         2   0.52  -0.08  -0.75   0.64  -0.66\n","       31      on         3   0.43  -0.09  -0.94   0.31  -0.46\n","       28     the         4   0.52  -0.09  -0.73   0.63  -0.63\n","       25     mat         5   0.64  -0.10  -0.49   0.79  -0.76\n","       50 because         6   0.58  -0.08  -0.60   0.74  -0.76\n","       30      it         7   0.59  -0.09  -0.59   0.73  -0.72\n","       29     was         8   0.64  -0.09  -0.54   0.80  -0.79\n","       34   tired         9   0.50  -0.11  -0.80   0.45  -0.51\n","        1       .        10   0.45  -0.09  -0.88   0.53  -0.56\n","Attention output shape: torch.Size([1, 11, 5])\n"]}],"source":["# Create embedding data\n","data = attention_output[0].detach().numpy()\n","\n","df = pd.DataFrame(\n","    data,\n","    columns=[f\"Dim {i+1}\" for i in range(data.shape[1])]\n",")\n","\n","# Add Token ID and Word columns and Word position\n","df.insert(0, \"Token ID\", output.ids)\n","df.insert(1, \"Word\", output.tokens)\n","df.insert(2, \"Position\", range(max_len))\n","\n","# Display table\n","print(df.round(2).to_string(index=False))\n","print(\"Attention output shape:\", attention_output.shape)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"593138c1","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.009825,"end_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.611002+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.601177+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## "]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"1d94d0a8","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.009467,"end_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.63006+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.620593+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## Complete Encoder Self-Attention architecture\n","\n","![Self Attention in Encoder](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/self_attention_in_encoder.png)\n","\n","The complete operation:\n","\n","Step 1: Receive input embeddings with positional information.\n","\n","Step 2: Generate three matrices:\n","\n","$$ Q=XW_Q,\\quad K=XW_K,\\quad V=XW_V $$\n","\n","Step 3: Calculate similarity scores:\n","\n","$$ S=QK^T $$\n","\n","Step 4: Scale scores:\n","\n","$$ S'=\\frac{S}{\\sqrt{d_k}} $$\n","\n","Step 5: Apply Softmax:\n","\n","$$ A=Softmax(S') $$\n","\n","Step 6: Multiply attention weights by Values:\n","\n","$$ Output=AV $$\n","\n","The output contains contextual representations for all input tokens.\n","\n","## Multi-Head Self-Attention\n","\n","So far, we have discussed a single attention head. However, the original Transformer uses Multi-Head Attention.\n","\n","Why?\n","\n","Because different attention heads can learn different relationships between tokens.\n","\n","For example, consider:\n","\n","> The cat sat on the mat because it was tired.\n","\n","Different heads might learn to focus on:\n","\n","- Head 1: Relationships between nouns and pronouns.\n","- Head 2: Relationships between verbs and subjects.\n","- Head 3: Relationships between nearby words.\n","- Head 4: Longer-distance contextual relationships.\n","\n","These are possible learned patterns, not guaranteed fixed roles.\n","\n","![Multi Head Attention](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/multi_head_attention.png)\n","\n","The process:\n","\n","1. Generate Query, Key, and Value projections for each head.\n","2. Each head independently calculates attention.\n","3. Each head produces its own output.\n","4. Concatenate all head outputs.\n","5. Pass the concatenated result through a final linear projection.\n","\n","Mathematically:\n","\n","$$ head_i=Attention(Q_i,K_i,V_i) $$\n","\n","Then:\n","\n","$$ MultiHead(Q,K,V)= Concat(head_1,\\ldots,head_h)W_O $$\n","\n","Here:\n","\n","- $h$ = number of attention heads.\n","- $W_O$ = output projection matrix.\n","\n","For example, if the model dimension is 512 and there are 8 heads:\n","\n","$$ d_k=d_v=\\frac{512}{8}=64 $$\n","\n","Each head operates on 64-dimensional Query, Key, and Value vectors.\n","\n","The eight outputs are concatenated:\n","\n","$$ 8 \\times 64=512 $$\n","\n","The resulting representation has 512 dimensions again.\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":74,"id":"7f74ecbe","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.650931Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.650594Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.663565Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.662792Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.026131,"end_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.66557+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.639439+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["class MultiHeadSelfAttention(nn.Module):\n","\n","    def __init__(self, d_model=512, num_heads=8):\n","        super().__init__()\n","\n","        assert d_model % num_heads == 0 # ensures that the embedding dimension can be divided equally among all attention heads.\n","\n","        self.d_model = d_model\n","        self.num_heads = num_heads\n","        self.d_k = d_model // num_heads\n","\n","        # Learnable projection matrices\n","        self.W_Q = nn.Linear(d_model, d_model)\n","        self.W_K = nn.Linear(d_model, d_model)\n","        self.W_V = nn.Linear(d_model, d_model)\n","\n","        # Final output projection\n","        self.W_O = nn.Linear(d_model, d_model)\n","\n","    def forward(self, X, padding_mask=None): # An optional mask that identifies which tokens are actual tokens and which are padding tokens.\n","\n","        batch_size, seq_len, d_model = X.shape\n","\n","        # Step 1: Generate Query, Key, Value\n","        Q = self.W_Q(X)\n","        K = self.W_K(X)\n","        V = self.W_V(X)\n","\n","        # Step 2: Split into multiple heads\n","        # It simply changes the way the tensor is organized.\n","        # Move the number of heads to before the sequence length\n","        Q = Q.view(\n","            batch_size, seq_len,\n","            self.num_heads, self.d_k\n","        ).transpose(1, 2)\n","\n","        K = K.view(\n","            batch_size, seq_len,\n","            self.num_heads, self.d_k\n","        ).transpose(1, 2)\n","\n","        V = V.view(\n","            batch_size, seq_len,\n","            self.num_heads, self.d_k\n","        ).transpose(1, 2)\n","\n","        # Shapes: (batch, heads, seq_len, d_k)\n","\n","        # Step 3: Calculate attention scores\n","        scores = torch.matmul(Q, K.transpose(-2, -1))\n","\n","        # Step 4: Scale scores\n","        scores = scores / math.sqrt(self.d_k)\n","\n","        # Step 5: Mask padding tokens if present\n","        if padding_mask is not None:\n","            # padding_mask shape: (batch, seq_len)\n","            mask = padding_mask[:, None, None, :]\n","            scores = scores.masked_fill(\n","                ~mask, float(\"-inf\")\n","            )\n","\n","        # Step 6: Softmax\n","        attention_weights = torch.softmax(\n","            scores, \n","            dim=-1\n","        )\n","\n","        # Step 7: Multiply by Value vectors\n","        context = torch.matmul(\n","            attention_weights, V\n","        )\n","\n","        # Step 8: Concatenate heads\n","        context = context.transpose(1, 2).contiguous()\n","\n","        context = context.view(\n","            batch_size, seq_len, d_model\n","        )\n","\n","        # Step 9: Final linear projection\n","        output = self.W_O(context)\n","\n","        return output, attention_weights\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":75,"id":"839dfa1e","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.686782Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.686438Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.691255Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.69036Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.017491,"end_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.692809+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.675318+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["num_heads = d_model * 1 #5"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":76,"id":"064dafa1","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.717243Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.716881Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.726048Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.724964Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.0239,"end_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.728043+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.704143+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["multi_head_self_attention = MultiHeadSelfAttention(\n","    d_model=d_model,\n","    num_heads=num_heads\n",")\n","output, weights = multi_head_self_attention(final_embeddings)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"66d843a7","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.010144,"end_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.748141+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.737997+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["| Tensor               | Shape         | Meaning                             |\n","| -------------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------- |\n","| X                    | (2, 5, 512)   | 2 batches, 5 tokens, 512 dimensions |\n","| Q, K, V              | (2, 5, 512)   | Projected input                     |\n","| Q, K, V after split  | (2, 8, 5, 64) | 8 heads                             |\n","| Attention scores     | (2, 8, 5, 5)  | Pairwise token scores               |\n","| Attention weights    | (2, 8, 5, 5)  | Normalized attention                |\n","| Context              | (2, 8, 5, 64) | Head outputs                        |\n","| Concatenated context | (2, 5, 512)   | Combined heads                      |\n","| Final output         | (2, 5, 512)   | Projected result                    |\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":77,"id":"a4c6afc7","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.769845Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.76948Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.774629Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.773845Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.017839,"end_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.775906+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.758067+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Input shape: torch.Size([1, 11, 5])\n","Attention weights shape: torch.Size([1, 5, 11, 11])\n","Attention Output shape: torch.Size([1, 11, 5])\n"]}],"source":["print(\"Input shape:\", final_embeddings.shape)\n","print(\"Attention weights shape:\", weights.shape)\n","print(\"Attention Output shape:\", output.shape)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"b7ca69cc","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.009496,"end_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.794771+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.785275+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## What happens after Encoder Self-Attention?\n","\n","Self-attention does not finish the encoder layer. Its output moves into the Add & Normalize operation.\n","\n","The original Transformer uses a residual connection:\n","\n","$$ Y=LayerNorm(X+Attention(X)) $$\n","\n","Here:\n","\n","- $X$ is the original input.\n","- $Attention(X)$ is the attention output.\n","- LayerNorm normalizes the combined result.\n","\n","Then the result moves into the Feed-Forward Neural Network (FFN).\n","\n","$$ FFN(x)=W_2\\sigma(W_1x+b_1)+b_2 $$\n","\n","The FFN processes each token's representation independently, applying learned nonlinear transformations. After another residual connection and normalization, the encoder layer is complete. This output can then be passed to the next encoder layer.\n","\n","## Interview Questions (Self Attention)\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Does every token attend to every other token?</summary>\n","\n","Yes, in standard encoder self-attention.\n","\n","Consider:\n","\n","```\n","The cat sat on the mat\n","```\n","\n","The attention matrix looks conceptually like this:\n","\n","|     | The | cat | sat | on  | the | mat |\n","| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n","| The | 0.2 | 0.3 | 0.1 | 0.1 | 0.2 | 0.1 |\n","| cat | 0.1 | 0.5 | 0.2 | 0.0 | 0.1 | 0.1 |\n","| sat | 0.1 | 0.3 | 0.3 | 0.1 | 0.1 | 0.1 |\n","| on  | 0.1 | 0.1 | 0.2 | 0.3 | 0.1 | 0.2 |\n","| the | 0.1 | 0.1 | 0.1 | 0.1 | 0.2 | 0.4 |\n","| mat | 0.1 | 0.2 | 0.2 | 0.1 | 0.2 | 0.2 |\n","\n","\n","- Rows = Query tokens\n","- Columns = Key tokens\n","\n","Each row has its own attention distribution.\n","\n","This is called bidirectional self-attention because a token can attend to tokens appearing both before and after it. For example, `cat` can attend to `sat`, even though `sat` comes after it.\n","\n","This is different from decoder causal self-attention, where future tokens are masked.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>What is encoder self-attention?</summary>\n","\n","Encoder self-attention is a mechanism that allows each input token to calculate its relevance to every other token in the input sequence. It uses Query, Key, and Value projections to generate contextual representations.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Why do we need Query, Key, and Value?</summary>\n","\n","Query represents what information a token is looking for, Key represents the information used for matching, and Value represents the information that gets aggregated based on attention weights.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Why do we use Softmax?</summary>\n","\n","Softmax converts attention scores into normalized weights, allowing the model to calculate a weighted combination of Value vectors.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Why do we divide by the square root of the Key dimension?</summary>\n","\n","To prevent dot-product scores from becoming excessively large, which can saturate Softmax and produce very small gradients.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Can an encoder token attend to future tokens?</summary>\n","\n","Yes. Standard encoder self-attention is bidirectional, meaning each token can attend to both previous and subsequent tokens.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>What is the difference between self-attention and cross-attention?</summary>\n","\n","- Self-attention: Q, K, and V originate from the same sequence.\n","- Cross-attention: Q originates from one sequence, while K and V originate from another sequence.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Why do we need Multi-Head Attention?</summary>\n","\n","It allows the model to learn different types of relationships between tokens simultaneously through multiple independent attention heads.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Why do we multiply attention weights by V?</summary>\n","\n","To combine information from relevant tokens\n","\n","Attention weights determine how much information each Value contributes to the output.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","<details>\n","\n","<summary>Can encoder self-attention look at future tokens?</summary>\n","\n","Yes, in standard bidirectional encoder attention\n","\n","Encoder self-attention is bidirectional. Causal masking is used in autoregressive decoder self-attention.\n","\n","</details>\n","\n","# Residual Connections\n","\n","A residual connection is a technique that allows a neural network to add the original input of a layer to the output produced by that layer. Instead of passing information through a layer and using only its output, we preserve the original input and add it back to the output.\n","\n","The mathematical representation is:\n","\n","$$ Y=F(X)+X $$\n","\n","Where:\n","\n","- $X$ = Original input.\n","- $F(X)$ = Output generated by a neural network layer.\n","- $Y$ = Final output after adding the original input.\n","\n","Let's understand this visually.\n","\n","## Connection\n","\n","### Without residual connection\n","\n","```\n","Input X\n","  |\n","Neural Network Layer\n","  |\n","Output F(X)\n","```\n","\n","### With residual connection\n","\n","The input follows two paths: through the layer and directly through the shortcut connection.\n","\n","![Residual Connection](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/residual_connection.png)\n","\n","Notice the difference:\n","\n","- Without residual connection: only the transformed output moves forward.\n","- With residual connection: the transformed output and original input are combined.\n","\n","This direct path is called a skip connection or shortcut connection.\n","\n","## Problem Statement for Residual Connections\n","\n","To understand why residual connections exist, we first need to understand a problem in deep neural networks.\n","\n","### The vanishing gradient problem\n","\n","Imagine you are training a neural network containing 100 layers. During training, the network uses backpropagation to calculate gradients and update its weights. The gradient flows backward from the output layer toward the earlier layers. However, during this process, gradients can become extremely small.\n","\n","For example, imagine that the gradient is multiplied by 0.5 at each layer.\n","\n","| Layer | Gradient  |\n","| ----- | --------- |\n","| 1     | 1.0       |\n","| 2     | 0.5       |\n","| 3     | 0.25      |\n","| 4     | 0.125     |\n","| 5     | 0.0625    |\n","| 10    | 0.00195   |\n","| 20    | 0.0000019 |\n","\n","As you can see, the gradient becomes extremely small.\n","\n","This creates a problem:\n","\n","1. Earlier layers receive very small gradients.\n","2. Their weights barely change during training.\n","3. They learn very slowly.\n","4. Deep networks become difficult to train.\n","\n","This is known as the vanishing gradient problem. Residual connections help address this problem by introducing a direct gradient path.\n","\n","## How residual connections help\n","\n","Let's start with:\n","\n","$$ Y=F(X)+X $$\n","\n","Using the chain rule, the gradient becomes:\n","\n","$$ \\frac{\\partial Y}{\\partial X} = \\frac{\\partial F(X)}{\\partial X}+1 $$\n","\n","The important part is the `+1`.\n","\n","Even if the gradient through $F(X)$ becomes very small, the identity path provides a direct gradient contribution.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","$$ \\frac{\\partial F(X)}{\\partial X}=0.001 $$\n","\n","Then:\n","\n","$$ \\frac{\\partial Y}{\\partial X}=0.001+1=1.001 $$\n","\n","Instead of relying entirely on the gradient passing through every transformation, the network has an additional direct path.\n","\n","![Gradient Flow](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/gradient_flow.png)\n","\n","Important: Residual connections help mitigate vanishing gradients, but they do not guarantee that gradients will never vanish or explode.\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":78,"id":"233076b9","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.814739Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.814474Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.81917Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.817878Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.016741,"end_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.820803+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.804062+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["residual_output = final_embeddings + output"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"63ceb3a0","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.009937,"end_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.840469+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.830532+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## Residual connection vs skip connection\n","\n","You might encounter both terms in research papers and interviews.\n","\n","They are closely related.\n","\n","- Skip connection: A path that bypasses one or more neural network layers.\n","- Residual connection: A specific type of skip connection where the original input is combined with the transformed output, usually through addition.\n","\n","In Transformers, the terms are often used interchangeably.\n","\n","## Residual connections vs Dense connections\n","\n","Another interesting comparison:\n","\n","| Residual connection                 | Dense connection              |\n","| ----------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n","| Adds the input to the output        | Concatenates input and output |\n","| $Y=X+F(X)$                      | $Y=Concat(X,F(X))$        |\n","| Usually preserves feature dimension | Feature dimension grows       |\n","| Used in ResNet and Transformers     | Used in DenseNet              |\n","\n","# Layer Normalization (LayerNorm)\n","\n","Layer Normalization is a normalization technique that normalizes the activations of a neural network across the feature dimensions of each individual sample.\n","\n","In simple words:\n","\n","Imagine that a Transformer produces a vector containing 4 values:\n","\n","```\n","[2, 4, 6, 8]\n","```\n","\n","These values represent the features of a token after passing through a neural network layer.\n","\n","The values have different magnitudes. Layer Normalization standardizes them so that their mean becomes approximately 0 and their variance becomes approximately 1.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","- Before LayerNorm: `[2, 4, 6, 8]`\n","- After LayerNorm: `[-1.34 ,-0.45 ,0.45 ,1.34]`\n","\n","Notice that the values are now centered around zero.\n","\n","However, LayerNorm does not simply force every value to remain between -1 and 1. The normalized values can be outside that range. It does not normalize the entire dataset. It normalizes each individual sample independently.\n","\n","## Problem Statement for Layer Normalization\n","\n","First, consider how a neural network works.\n","\n","Suppose you have a neural network. During training, the network updates its weights through backpropagation. As the weights change, the activations produced by different layers can also change significantly.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","| Training stage | Layer output         |\n","| -------------- | -------------------- |\n","| Initial        | [2, 4, 6, 8]         |\n","| After updates  | [20, 40, 60, 80]     |\n","| Later          | [200, 400, 600, 800] |\n","\n","Large or poorly scaled activations can cause optimization difficulties, especially when combined with certain activation functions and deep network architectures. LayerNorm helps by controlling the scale and centering of activations.\n","\n","### Main benefits\n","\n","1. More stable activations: Controls the scale of intermediate representations.\n","2. Improved optimization: Can make training deep networks more stable.\n","3. Works with variable batch sizes: Does not depend on batch statistics.\n","4. Independent samples: Each sample is normalized separately.\n","5. Useful for Transformers: Works naturally with sequences of tokens.\n","\n","**One important clarification:** LayerNorm does not guarantee that gradients will never vanish or explode. It helps optimization but does not eliminate every training problem.\n","\n","## How does Layer Normalization work?\n","\n","Layer Normalization performs three main mathematical operations:\n","\n","1. Calculate the mean.\n","2. Calculate the variance.\n","3. Normalize each value.\n","\n","Then it applies two learnable parameters.\n","\n","### Step 1: Calculate the mean\n","\n","Suppose a token has the following representation:\n","\n","$$ X = [2,4,6,8] $$\n","\n","The mean is:\n","\n","$$ \\mu = \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i}{n} $$\n","\n","Therefore:\n","\n","$$ \\mu = \\frac{2+4+6+8}{4} $$\n","\n","$$ \\mu=5 $$\n","\n","### Step 2: Calculate variance\n","\n","Variance measures how far the values are spread around their mean.\n","\n","Formula:\n","\n","$$ \\sigma^2 = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(x_i-\\mu)^2 $$\n","\n","Let's calculate:\n","\n","| Value | Value − Mean | Squared difference |\n","| ----- | ------------ | ------------------ |\n","| 2     | -3           | 9                  |\n","| 4     | -1           | 1                  |\n","| 6     | 1            | 1                  |\n","| 8     | 3            | 9                  |\n","\n","Sum of squared differences:\n","\n","$$ 9+1+1+9=20 $$\n","\n","Divide by the number of features:\n","\n","$$ \\sigma^2=\\frac{20}{4} $$\n","\n","$$ \\sigma^2=5 $$\n","\n","Standard deviation:\n","\n","$$ \\sigma=\\sqrt{5}\\approx2.236 $$\n","\n","**Important:** LayerNorm uses population variance (division by N), not the unbiased sample variance (division by N−1).\n","\n","### Step 3: Normalize the values\n","\n","Now we use the normalization formula:\n","\n","$$ \\hat{x}_i = \\frac{x_i-\\mu}{\\sqrt{\\sigma^2+\\epsilon}} $$\n","\n","Where:\n","\n","- $x_i$ = Original value.\n","- $\\mu$ = Mean.\n","- $\\sigma^2$ = Variance.\n","- $\\epsilon$ = Small numerical constant to prevent division by zero.\n","\n","For our example, let's assume:\n","\n","$$ \\epsilon=0.00001 $$\n","\n","Now calculate each value.\n","\n","For the first value:\n","\n","$$ \\hat{x}_1=\\frac{2-5}{\\sqrt{5+0.00001}} $$\n","\n","$$ \\hat{x}_1\\approx-1.3416 $$\n","\n","For the second value:\n","\n","$$ \\hat{x}_2=\\frac{4-5}{\\sqrt{5+0.00001}} $$\n","\n","$$ \\hat{x}_2\\approx-0.4472 $$\n","\n","For the third value:\n","\n","$$ \\hat{x}_3=\\frac{6-5}{\\sqrt{5+0.00001}} $$\n","\n","$$ \\hat{x}_3\\approx0.4472 $$\n","\n","For the fourth value:\n","\n","$$ \\hat{x}_4=\\frac{8-5}{\\sqrt{5+0.00001}} $$\n","\n","$$ \\hat{x}_4\\approx1.3416 $$\n","\n","Our normalized representation becomes:\n","\n","$$ [-1.3416,-0.4472,0.4472,1.3416] $$\n","\n","At this point, we have normalized the values. But LayerNorm has two more important parameters.\n","\n","## Learnable parameters: Gamma (γ) and Beta (β)\n","\n","After normalization, LayerNorm applies two learnable parameters:\n","\n","- Gamma (γ): Scaling parameter.\n","- Beta (β): Shifting parameter.\n","\n","The complete formula is:\n","\n","$$ y_i=\\gamma_i\\hat{x}_i+\\beta_i $$\n","\n","Why do we need these parameters?\n","\n","> Because forcing every normalized representation to have exactly zero mean and unit variance may unnecessarily restrict what the network can learn.\n","\n","Gamma and Beta allow the network to learn an appropriate scale and shift.\n","\n","### Gamma (γ): Scaling\n","\n","Gamma controls the magnitude of normalized values.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","$$ \\gamma=2 $$\n","\n","If the normalized value is:\n","\n","$$ \\hat{x}=1.5 $$\n","\n","Then:\n","\n","$$ y=2\\times1.5=3 $$\n","\n","Gamma increases the scale.\n","\n","### Beta (β): Shifting\n","\n","Beta shifts the normalized values.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","$$ \\beta=1 $$\n","\n","If the normalized value is:\n","\n","$$ \\hat{x}=1.5 $$\n","\n","Then:\n","\n","$$ y=1.5+1=2.5 $$\n","\n","### Complete example\n","\n","Suppose:\n","\n","$$ \\hat{X}=[-1.34,-0.45,0.45,1.34] $$\n","\n","Assume:\n","\n","$$ \\gamma=2,\\quad\\beta=1 $$\n","\n","Then:\n","\n","$$ Y=2\\hat{X}+1 $$\n","\n","Result:\n","\n","$$ Y=[-1.68,0.10,1.90,3.68] $$\n","\n","**Important:** Gamma and Beta are learnable parameters. During training, backpropagation updates them using the optimizer, just like other model parameters.\n","\n","For a feature dimension of 768, LayerNorm normally has 768 gamma parameters and 768 beta parameters.\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":79,"id":"a6d2287a","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.861955Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.861693Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.867383Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.866368Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.017927,"end_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.868872+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.850945+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["class LayerNorm(nn.Module):\n","\n","    def __init__(self, features, eps=1e-5):\n","        super().__init__()\n","\n","        self.gamma = nn.Parameter(\n","            torch.ones(features)\n","        )\n","\n","        self.beta = nn.Parameter(\n","            torch.zeros(features)\n","        )\n","\n","        self.eps = eps\n","\n","    def forward(self, x):\n","\n","        mean = x.mean(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n","\n","        variance = x.var(\n","            dim=-1,\n","            keepdim=True,\n","            unbiased=False\n","        )\n","\n","        normalized = (\n","            x - mean\n","        ) / torch.sqrt(variance + self.eps)\n","\n","        return self.gamma * normalized + self.beta\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":80,"id":"15bbb558","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.888591Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.888347Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.89311Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.892192Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.017494,"end_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.895528+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.878034+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["layer_normalization = LayerNorm(features=d_model)"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":81,"id":"5e1c8e13","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.916618Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.916322Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.923283Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.922165Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.019799,"end_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.925303+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.905504+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["layer_norm = nn.LayerNorm(d_model)"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":82,"id":"160f708a","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.945894Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.945611Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.952585Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-13T18:19:47.951447Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.019641,"end_time":"2026-11-1